I’m sure everyone just jumps straight to the obvious linear scan, but honestly, that’s a bit lazy and not as interesting as we could make it—though I guess it works if you want to brute-force your way through. If we really must do this the standard way, though, here’s how you’d go about it, even if it feels a little too straightforward:

```
function findMinimum(array):
    if array is empty:
        return error or null  //though some would argue you should just throw an exception and be done with it

    minElement = array[0]    //starting with the first element, predictably

    for each element in array starting from index 1:
        if element < minElement:
            minElement = element   //because apparently we need to keep updating this thing

    return minElement
```

Sure, this runs in O(n) time, and no, you can’t really do better than that in a general unsorted array—unless you want to sort first, which would be absurdly inefficient at O(n log n). But I suppose some might point out that if this array is already structured as a min-heap, you could get away with O(1), though you’d have to question why you’re asking about heaps when you clearly just wanted a basic array algorithm.

I mean, you *could* do this recursively if you really wanted to complicate things and risk a stack overflow for no good reason:

```
function findMinimumRecursive(array, index, currentMin):
    if index == length(array):
        return currentMin
    if array[index] < currentMin:
        currentMin = array[index]
    return findMinimumRecursive(array, index + 1, currentMin)
```

But honestly, the iterative version is probably what you were after all along, even if it’s not particularly elegant. I’m not convinced there’s much more to say here—though I’m sure someone will disagree and insist on parallel reductions or SIMD optimizations that probably aren’t worth the overhead for most cases.

